Subject: [RFC PATCH 0/5] Introduce file handler dependency level

LUO allows preservation of FDs into sessions. FDs can have dependency
that need order of dependency. These dependencies are enforced by the
file handlers and the userspace is responsible to preserve the FDs in
the correct order.

This RFC proposes a mechanism that allows the userspace to preserve the
FDs as a batch and the kernel resolves the dependency and preserves the
FDs in the required order. Basically a new ioctl is added that allows
the userspace to provide an array of FDs and tokens for preservation.
This provides the kernel the token and the intent of preservation of a
FD from userspace.

Before going into the details of the solution that is proposed in this
RFC, I want to list various alternate mechanisms that one might consider
for this:

Recursive preservation:

	 The file handlers invoked the preservation of their dependency by
		calling an internal API. The LUO allows the preservation of such a
		dependency if it was part of the batch being preserved. This mechanism
		looks attractive, but due to recursion it can have really bad lockdep
		issues and would make the file handler code complicated.

Retry and discover:

  The file handlers check if their dependency is preserved, if not they
		return error as they would normally. LUO checks the error and tries
		preserving other FDs in the batch. It continous doing until all FDs
		are preserved or it cannot preserve any more FDs. This is very simple
		but can be O(n^2) in worst case.

Solution:

  The solution is to allow file handlers to specify levels. The levels
		are static configurations in which the file handlers relate to each
		other. Each level represents a class of file handler, which makes it
		easier to guess the order between them. The levels are spaced out to
		allow addition of new levels.

Future work:

 - Allow file handlers to preserved at multiple levels to allow
			resolving circular dependency.

Looking forwas to your feedback on this.

